The emerald cut diamond GIA vs IGI question matters more for this shape than for almost any other cut. Here is why: the emerald cut's open step-cut table makes every grade visible.

A VS2 clarity inclusion you cannot see in a round brilliant is often visible in an emerald cut. If your certificate overstates the grade, you find out when the stone arrives.

GIA and IGI are both real certification bodies. They do not both grade the same way.

This guide covers what each lab actually tests, why IGI lab-grown grades run 1–2 grades higher than GIA for the same stone, what that costs you at every carat weight, and when each certification is the right choice.

---

**TL;DR**

- For natural emerald cut diamonds: GIA only. The open step-cut table makes overstated grades visible, and IGI grades natural stones inconsistently.
- For lab-grown emerald cut diamonds: IGI is the industry standard, used by most retailers including Blue Nile. Understand The Grade Bump before you compare prices.
- The Grade Bump: IGI grades lab stones 1–2 color and clarity grades higher than GIA for the same stone. A D-VS1 IGI lab stone is often an F-VS2 by GIA standards.
- → Natural 1ct GIA D-VS1 from [$3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). → Lab 2ct IGI D-FL from [$4,735](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28828463?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).
- Priority: match the certification to the stone type. GIA for natural. IGI for lab — with eyes open to the grade adjustment.

---

## Expert Summary

GIA (Gemological Institute of America) and IGI (International Gemological Institute) both issue grading certificates for loose diamonds. GIA is the older and more conservative lab. IGI is the larger lab by volume and dominates the lab-grown market.

For natural emerald cut diamonds, GIA is the only certification worth buying. GIA natural diamond grading is consistent, globally recognized, and is the benchmark used for resale pricing. IGI natural grading has historically been less consistent, and an IGI-certified natural stone typically trades at a discount on the secondary market.

For lab-grown diamonds, IGI is acceptable and widely used. Most Blue Nile lab-grown emerald cuts carry IGI or GCAL certificates.

The caveat: IGI grades lab-grown stones 1–2 grades higher on color and clarity than GIA grades the same stone. This is not fraud — IGI's grading standard for lab stones is simply more lenient. But it means you cannot compare a D-VS1 IGI lab stone directly to a D-VS1 GIA lab stone and expect the same visual quality.

The step-cut geometry of the emerald cut amplifies this risk. An overstated clarity grade shows in the open table. An overstated color grade shows in white gold and platinum settings.

The certificate matters more here than in a round brilliant.

---

## What GIA and IGI Actually Test

Both labs examine the same four factors: carat weight, color, clarity, and cut. The process is similar: the stone is submitted, examined by multiple graders, and a certificate is issued with grades assigned.

The differences are in calibration and strictness.

**GIA** grades on the strictest color and clarity scale in the industry. Multiple independent graders assess each stone, and the grades are reconciled before the certificate is issued. GIA does not disclose its internal process in detail, but its reputation is built on consistency — a D-VS1 from GIA in 2005 is comparable to a D-VS1 from GIA today.

GIA was slower to enter the lab-grown market. They initially issued origin reports rather than full grading reports for lab stones.

They now issue full certificates for lab-grown diamonds, but their volume is a fraction of IGI's.

**IGI** was founded in 1975 and is based in Antwerp. It has the highest volume of any grading lab globally. For natural diamonds, IGI grading has been historically less consistent than GIA, which is why natural IGI stones trade at a discount.

For lab-grown diamonds, IGI moved aggressively and became the dominant certification body.

Most lab-grown diamonds sold at Blue Nile, James Allen, and other major retailers carry IGI certificates.

**GCAL** (Gem Certification and Assurance Lab) is a smaller US-based lab that offers a full grading report plus a light performance certificate. It grades lab-grown stones at a standard between IGI's leniency and GIA's strictness. GCAL is the third option worth considering.

![GIA vs IGI vs GCAL grading lab comparison — emerald cut diamond certification standards](/images/emerald-cut-diamond-gia-igi-gcal-comparison.avif)

---

## The Natural Diamond Rule

For natural emerald cut diamonds, GIA is the only certification that matters. This is not a preference — it is a market reality.

When you buy a natural diamond, you are buying it partly for resale value. Resale pricing for natural diamonds is based on GIA grades. An IGI-graded natural stone does not carry the same resale value as an equivalent GIA-graded stone.

Diamond dealers and auction houses use GIA grading as the benchmark. An IGI certificate on a natural stone raises questions that a GIA certificate does not.

The emerald cut makes this more important, not less. The step-cut table is an open window. If the clarity grade is overstated by one grade — VS1 on the certificate, VS2 in reality — the inclusion is visible at arm's length.

In a round brilliant, VS2 inclusions are nearly always eye-clean. In an emerald cut, VS2 sometimes shows.

