All natural emerald cuts in certified market inventory above use GIA grading. This is not a coincidence — reputable retailers do not promote IGI-graded natural stones at the same level.

If you find a natural emerald cut at a noticeably lower price with an IGI certificate, the discount exists for a reason. Either the IGI grade is more generous than a GIA grade would be, or the stone has characteristics that would not survive GIA's stricter review. Do not buy a natural emerald cut on an IGI certificate unless you have it independently appraised by a GIA-certified gemologist first.

> **Farzana says:** "I have seen natural IGI stones that were beautiful. I have also seen IGI VS1 stones that were clearly VS2 by any honest standard. The inconsistency is the problem. With GIA, I know what I am looking at. With IGI on a natural stone, I have to verify every single grade independently. That defeats the purpose of paying for a certificate."

---

## The Grade Bump

This is the most important thing to understand about IGI lab-grown grading.

IGI grades lab-grown diamonds on a more lenient scale than GIA. The difference is not random — it is systematic. Across thousands of stones, IGI lab-grown grades run approximately 1–2 color grades and 1 clarity grade above what GIA would assign to the same stone.

A stone IGI grades as D color is frequently F or G by GIA standards. A stone IGI grades as VS1 clarity is frequently VS2 or SI1 by GIA standards.

This is called the Grade Bump — and it has a direct price consequence.

![Emerald cut diamond IGI vs GIA grade bump — lab-grown color and clarity grade comparison](/images/emerald-cut-diamond-igi-grade-bump-comparison.avif)

When you compare a D-VS1 IGI lab stone to a D-VS1 GIA lab stone, you are not comparing equivalent stones. The GIA D-VS1 has been graded by the stricter standard. It will look better, particularly in the open table of an emerald cut.

**How to adjust for the Grade Bump when buying IGI lab:**
- Treat IGI D-E color as equivalent to GIA F-G color when evaluating appearance
- Treat IGI VVS1-VVS2 clarity as equivalent to GIA VS1-VS2 when evaluating visibility
- Do not buy IGI SI1 in an emerald cut expecting eye-clean results — step facets make SI1 inclusions visible
- When comparing prices, compare IGI D-VS1 to GIA F-VS2, not to GIA D-VS1

**Grade Bump Quick Reference — Color:**

