## Why Emerald Cut Makes Certification More Important

Most cut guides discuss GIA vs IGI in the context of round brilliants. The stakes are lower for brilliant cuts because the 58 facets scatter light aggressively — inclusions hide and color disperses.

A VS2 round brilliant is almost always eye-clean. An H-color round looks white in isolation.

The emerald cut has none of these advantages.

The step-cut table is flat and transparent. Light enters, reflects off the pavilion facets in long slow planes, and exits. Inclusions in the table view are visible.

Body color shows as warmth in the clear, unbroken reflections.

**What this means for certification grades:**
- A clarity grade that is overstated by one step (VS1 → actual VS2) is often visible in an emerald cut table
- A color grade overstated by two grades (D → actual F-G) shows warmth in the stone under natural light
- SI1 clarity in an emerald cut is almost never eye-clean — the open table makes SI1 inclusions visible

The Grade Bump matters here more than with any other cut. If an IGI D-VS1 is actually an F-VS2 by GIA standards, the visual difference in an emerald cut is meaningful. The same mismatch in a round brilliant might not matter at all.

![Emerald cut diamond open table — why step-cut facets make certification grade accuracy critical](/images/emerald-cut-diamond-open-table-certification.avif)

This is the core reason the Natural Diamond Rule exists: for natural stones where resale and grading consistency matter, and where the open table amplifies every grade discrepancy, GIA is the only safe choice.

---

## GCAL: The Third Option Worth Knowing

GCAL is not discussed often enough. It occupies a real position between IGI's lenient grading and GIA's strict standard.

GCAL was founded in 2001 and is based in New York. Its grading standard for lab-grown diamonds is stricter than IGI's. It also issues a light performance certificate alongside the grading report, which measures actual optical brilliance.

No other major lab does this.

For emerald cut diamonds specifically, the GCAL light performance report adds information that a standard clarity and color grade does not capture. Step-cut diamonds perform very differently from brilliant cuts in terms of how light moves through the stone. A GCAL certificate tells you how the stone actually performs, not just what grade the facets earned.

GCAL stones are less common than IGI stones in major retailer inventory. Blue Nile carries GCAL-certified lab emerald cuts at various carat weights. They typically price 10–20% above IGI equivalents at the same stated grade — reflecting the stricter grading and the additional light performance certificate.

For buyers who want stricter grading than IGI but cannot find a GIA-certified lab emerald cut at their target size, GCAL is the next-best option.

---

## How to Read a Certificate for an Emerald Cut Diamond

Whether GIA, IGI, or GCAL, the information on the certificate matters differently for a step-cut stone.

**Clarity plot location:** Check where the inclusion is marked on the clarity diagram. An inclusion at the center of the table is visible through the open emerald cut table. An inclusion at the edge or near the girdle is less visible. The same VS2 grade means different things depending on where the inclusion sits.

**Table and depth percentage:** GIA, IGI, and GCAL all report table % and depth % for emerald cuts. The ideal ranges are 61–69% table and 60–64% depth. Any stone outside these ranges may look different from what you expect.

**Polish and symmetry:** Both should be at least Good on any lab. Excellent is preferred. Poor polish or symmetry in a step-cut diamond is visible — the long flat facets amplify any imperfection in the finish.

**Fluorescence:** Medium or strong fluorescence in any certificate creates a risk of haziness in the emerald cut's open table under natural light. Stick to None or Faint fluorescence regardless of certificate issuer.

**Color and clarity grade adjustments by lab:**

<table class="dc-custom-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>IGI Lab Grade</th>
<th>Approximate GIA Equivalent</th>
<th>Approximate GCAL Equivalent</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>D color</td><td>F–G color</td><td>E–F color</td></tr>
<tr><td>E color</td><td>G–H color</td><td>F–G color</td></tr>
<tr><td>VVS1 clarity</td><td>VVS2–VS1 clarity</td><td>VS1 clarity</td></tr>
<tr><td>VS1 clarity</td><td>VS2 clarity</td><td>VS1–VS2 clarity</td></tr>
<tr><td>VS2 clarity</td><td>SI1 clarity</td><td>VS2 clarity</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

These are approximate ranges, not exact conversions. Individual stones vary. The table gives you a calibration framework — not a guarantee.

For any emerald cut above $5,000, request a video of the stone before purchasing to verify appearance independent of the certificate grade.

