---

## What the Certificate Does Not Tell You

The certificate is a grading document. It grades carat, color, clarity, and cut. It does not tell you several things that matter for an emerald cut purchase.

It does not tell you whether the stone looks good.

Two GIA D-VS1 emerald cuts can look completely different. One might have a long, elegant step pattern with beautiful hall-of-mirrors reflections. The other might have a crushed-ice appearance with choppy facet breaks.

Both are D-VS1 by GIA standards. The certificate cannot distinguish them.

Cut quality for emerald cuts is not graded the same way as for rounds. GIA gives round brilliants a single Cut grade from Excellent to Poor. For emerald cuts, there is no equivalent single cut grade — only the component grades for Polish and Symmetry.

The proportions table and depth percentage are listed, but the overall visual quality of the cut is something you can only assess from a video or in person.

Color appearance also varies beyond the grade. A D-color emerald cut in platinum will look different from the same grade in yellow gold. The certificate does not account for setting context.

If you are planning a yellow gold or rose gold setting, you can buy lower on the color scale — IGI F or G — without visual penalty, because the warm metal absorbs the color difference.

Fluorescence reactivity is listed on the certificate, but its actual visual impact depends on the stone. Some strongly fluorescent stones look crisp and bright. Others look hazed.

The only way to know is to see the specific stone under natural light — and request that the video be filmed in both indoor and outdoor lighting if possible.

The certificate tells you the grade. The video tells you the truth.

One more thing the certificate does not tell you: how the stone looks on a finger. Retailers sometimes provide ring simulation tools or photorealistic mockups, but these are always flattering. The best proxy for on-finger appearance is the stone's length-to-width ratio combined with the face-up millimeter dimensions.

A 2ct emerald cut at 8.5x6.2mm covers more finger length than a 2ct round at 8.1mm diameter. The certificate lists the millimeter dimensions — use them.

Buyers often spend more time reading the certificate than studying the video. The certificate creates an illusion of objectivity. But GIA can grade two emerald cut diamonds identically while one looks stunning and the other looks flat.

The certificate is a starting filter, not a buying decision. Pass through the filter quickly, then spend your time on the video. That is where the actual decision lives.

---

## Final Verdict

For natural emerald cut diamonds: GIA is not a preference — it is the only correct choice. The step-cut table makes every grade discrepancy visible. IGI natural grading is inconsistent.

The discount on an IGI natural stone reflects risk, not value.

For lab-grown emerald cut diamonds: IGI is fine if you understand the Grade Bump. Apply the 1–2 grade color adjustment and the 1 grade clarity adjustment before comparing prices across labs. GCAL gives you stricter grading and a light performance certificate — worth the 10–20% premium if grade accuracy matters to you.

The rule is simple. Match the certification standard to the stone type. Then let the video make the final call.

