---

## FAQs

### Is GIA or IGI better for an emerald cut diamond?

GIA is better for natural emerald cut diamonds. IGI is the industry standard for lab-grown emerald cuts and is acceptable if you understand the Grade Bump. For step-cut diamonds, where clarity and color are visible through the open table, grading accuracy matters more than for brilliant cuts.

### What is the Grade Bump in IGI lab diamond grading?

The Grade Bump refers to IGI's systematic tendency to grade lab-grown diamonds 1–2 color grades and 1 clarity grade higher than GIA would assign to the same stone. A D-VS1 IGI lab emerald cut is typically equivalent to an F-VS2 by GIA standards. The gap is consistent across the market.

### Is IGI a legitimate certification lab?

Yes. IGI is the largest grading lab in the world by volume and is the standard for lab-grown diamonds. Its certificates are real and accepted by major retailers globally.

The issue is not legitimacy — it is that IGI grades lab-grown stones on a more lenient scale than GIA.

### Can I buy an emerald cut diamond with an IGI natural certificate?

Not recommended. IGI natural grading has historically been inconsistent, and the discount on an IGI-certified natural stone versus a GIA-certified stone reflects lower buyer confidence. For natural diamonds, especially step-cut stones where grading accuracy is visible in the open table, GIA is the right choice.

### Does GIA certify lab-grown emerald cut diamonds?

Yes. GIA now issues full grading reports for lab-grown diamonds. However, GIA-certified lab stones are rare in major retailer inventory, typically priced 10–20% higher than IGI equivalents, and the GIA standard for lab stones does not provide the same resale benefit it does for natural stones.

### Is GCAL better than IGI for lab-grown emerald cuts?

In terms of grade accuracy, yes — GCAL grades lab stones more strictly than IGI. GCAL also includes a light performance certificate, which is useful for step-cut stones where light behavior matters. GCAL is priced 10–20% above IGI at the same stated grade, reflecting both stricter grading and the added certificate.

See the [2ct GCAL D-VVS1 lab emerald](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29639473?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at [$5,335](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29639473?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) as a reference point.

### Why does the emerald cut make certification matter more than other shapes?

The emerald cut's step-cut facets create an open, transparent table rather than the fragmented light pattern of brilliant cuts. Inclusions that would be invisible in a round brilliant are often visible in an emerald cut. Color that would be masked by sparkle in a round shows as warmth in the emerald's clear reflections.

An overstated grade on a certificate is more likely to produce a visible mismatch in an emerald cut than in any brilliant cut.

### What clarity grade should I buy for an emerald cut with an IGI certificate?

VVS1 or VVS2 on IGI is the practical minimum for an eye-clean result. IGI VVS1 is approximately GIA VS1. IGI VS1 is approximately GIA VS2 — which can show inclusions in the emerald cut's open table.

Do not buy IGI VS2 or SI1 in an emerald cut expecting eye-clean results.

### What color grade should I buy for an emerald cut with an IGI lab certificate?

D or E on IGI for white gold or platinum settings. IGI D color is approximately GIA F-G color. In platinum or white gold, a GIA G color is at the lower visible limit for emerald cuts — which means IGI D-E is the safe range.

In yellow gold, IGI F-G is acceptable since the warm metal offsets warmer color grades.

### Does the certification lab affect the price of an emerald cut lab diamond?

Yes, directly. IGI-certified lab stones are priced at the market baseline. GCAL-certified stones carry a 10–20% premium for the stricter grade.

GIA lab stones, when available, also carry a premium. The same size and stated grade will cost different amounts depending on the certificate, which is why comparing lab diamond prices across labs requires the Grade Bump adjustment.

### Should I request a video before buying a certified emerald cut?

Always, regardless of certificate. A certificate tells you the grade — a video shows you the actual appearance. For emerald cuts specifically, request a high-resolution loupe or microscope video that shows the stone face-up and in motion.

This reveals whether the clarity plot inclusions are visible through the table and whether the color reads warm or white. No certificate replaces seeing the stone.

### What is the minimum certificate I should accept for a lab emerald cut?

IGI, GCAL, or GIA. Do not buy a lab-grown emerald cut with a generic "appraisal" or an in-house store certificate. Appraisals assign replacement value, not grading accuracy.

For any stone above $1,000, one of these three laboratory certificates is the minimum.

### How does the step-cut clarity floor (VS1) interact with IGI grading?

The [step-cut clarity floor](/emerald-cut-diamond-clarity-guide) for emerald cuts is VS1 by GIA standards. Because IGI grades approximately one step higher, the effective IGI clarity floor for an eye-clean emerald cut is VVS2 or better. Buying an IGI VS1 lab emerald cut and expecting the same eye-clean result as a GIA VS1 is the most common certification error step-cut buyers make.

